from pathlib import Path
import json,hashlib
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parent;j=json.loads((R/'results.json').read_text())
rows=[]
for c in j['cases']:
 m=c['final_reused'];ci=c['bootstrap']['accuracy_95'];ref='official target' if 'lead_threshold' in c else 'Coinbase start-price check'
 rows.append('<tr>'+''.join('<td>'+str(v)+'</td>' for v in [c['minutes_elapsed'],ref,c.get('lead_threshold','none'),f"{100*m['accuracy']:.2f}%",m['active'],f"{100*m['coverage']:.1f}%",f'{100*ci[0]:.2f}–{100*ci[1]:.2f}%'])+'</tr>')
body='''<!doctype html><html><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Improving BTC interval prediction</title><style>body{font:16px/1.65 system-ui;max-width:1100px;margin:40px auto;padding:0 22px;color:#203046}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%}td,th{padding:10px;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;text-align:left}.lead{background:#edf2f7;border-radius:12px;padding:20px}</style></head><body><h1>Improve information, then test price advantage</h1><p>October 18, 2026 · User permits intramarket entry · Research only</p><p class="lead"><b>Entry timing and distance from the original target provide much more directional information than the indicator combinations tested so far.</b> At minute 10, following the current side of the target was 80.96% correct in the reused final sample. Requiring a normalized lead of at least 1 raised it to 93.15%, while covering 48.3% of markets. This is a retrospective diagnostic with no entry-price or profit model, not a demonstrated trading edge.</p><table><tr><th>Minute after open</th><th>Reference</th><th>Minimum normalized lead</th><th>Accuracy</th><th>Signals</th><th>Coverage</th><th>95% daily-block CI</th></tr>'''+''.join(rows)+'''</table><p>Final period: September 13–October 17 UTC, 3,320 official Kalshi outcomes. Same previously inspected dataset as earlier studies. Six fixed official-target cases plus two reference-price sensitivity checks; no fitted model or threshold optimization. These cases overlap and are not independent trades.</p><h2>What the lead filter means</h2><p>Normalized lead = absolute log(price / original contract target), divided by trailing volatility × square root of remaining time. Volatility uses the last 12 completed five-minute returns; elapsed decision time is either 5 or 10 minutes. A threshold of 1 means the current lead exceeds this heuristic remaining-move scale. It is not a calibrated probability. This tests the original 15-minute contract with only 10 or 5 minutes left, not a fresh 15-minute forecast.</p><h2>Why high accuracy does not establish profit</h2><p>A $1-payout contract with an 80% win probability loses 5 cents in expectation if purchased for 85 cents, even before fees. Expected value per share is probability of winning minus purchase price minus costs. Increasing the hit rate by buying likely winners can still lose money if the market charges enough.</p><h2>The next research design</h2><ol><li>Build a timestamped settlement-aware baseline: original contract strike, current CF BRTI, seconds remaining, trailing realized volatility, and the state of the final 60-second averaging window. Confirm historical availability of every input.</li><li>Benchmark against Kalshi's own executable bids/asks and implied probability, not only a 50% coin flip. Measure calibration, Brier score, and after-cost performance with a no-trade option.</li><li>Add information in small, separately evaluated groups: spot/perpetual aggressive trade flow and order-book imbalance; cross-venue price/basis differences; scheduled events and volatility regimes. Treat SMA/stochastic as optional features. Only retain a group if it improves forward evaluation beyond the price/strike/time baseline.</li><li>Start with a regularized probability model; compare one constrained tree model if useful. Choose features, thresholds and confidence-based abstention on past data only. Keep each entry minute as a separately specified policy. Do not select on the old final period.</li><li>Collect fresh prospective signals and executable quotes before making a performance claim. Record spread, fees, depth, quote age, latency and failures. Cluster validation by market/day, prevent repeated positions from masquerading as independent wins, and compare profit, drawdown, coverage and probability calibration.</li></ol><h2>Data access and limits</h2><p>A single public Kalshi historical-candle probe succeeded (HTTP 200, 15 one-minute candles with yes bid/ask fields). This confirms that one sample endpoint is accessible, not complete history or executable fills. Kalshi documents BRTI value feeds with 60-second averages, but authentication and, for REST passthrough, entitlement are required; actual account access has not been verified.</p><p>The diagnostic uses Coinbase completed candles and the original strike retrieved from final Kalshi metadata. Strike publication timestamps were not archived. Coinbase prices are not the CF BRTI settlement index or its 60-second average. Candle delivery latency, quote depth, executable entry prices and fees are not modeled. The independent scalar check verifies arithmetic and use of past candle timestamps, not historical data-publication or fill feasibility. No trading services changed.</p><p><a href="https://docs.kalshi.com/websockets/cfbenchmarks-value">Official BRTI feed documentation</a> · <a href="https://docs.kalshi.com/cfbenchmarks/rest-passthrough">BRTI REST access requirements</a> · <a href="https://docs.kalshi.com/api-reference/market/get-market-candlesticks">Historical bid/ask candle schema</a></p><p>Artifacts: EXPERIMENT.md, study.py, results.json, predictions.csv, verification.json, quote-access-probe.json and cloud-record.json.</p></body></html>'''
(R/'report.html').write_text(body)
(R/'manifest.json').write_text(json.dumps({p.name:hashlib.sha256(p.read_bytes()).hexdigest() for p in R.iterdir() if p.is_file() and p.name not in ('manifest.json','run.log')},indent=2))
